A1. When these were sent to France they were received in the best society. ~ CABLEGRAMS
A9. One of the numerous relatives in the Herring family. ~ SHAD
A13. A silk manufacturing concern. ~ COCOON
A14. This will change that acid disposition. ~ ALKALI
A16. A Scotch label. ~ MC
A18. To sharpen. ~ WHET
A19. This smells like spring. ~ LILAC
A20. The shortest route between Chicago and New York. ~ AIR
A22. This is a wise answer. ~ ORACLE
A26. Here is where a lot of stock was unloaded. ~ PORT
A27. This is a lot of nonsense. Some people call it Tommy. ~ ROT
A29. This is what the cook never did. ~ STAYED
A31. Even a Scotchman knows this is small. ~ SMA
A32. Prominent figures in the sporting world. ~ SCORE
A34. This is well known at Yale. ~ ELI
A35. Add this to your set and you will have a real asset. ~ AS
A37. A small specimen. ~ SP
A38. This society will never let anyone beat. ~ HUMANE
A40. It will be necessary to pass this in order to get in some colleges. ~ ENTRY
A42. When this gets in a "Judge" puzzle it must be either the humerus or the funny bone. ~ OS
A43. These need an introduction. ~ STRANGERS
A45. To perform. ~ DO
A46. Listened in. ~ HEARD
A47. Red tape in a department store. ~ NOTION
A49. This is not as large as fat Em. ~ EN
A51. This period of time has been shortened. ~ YR
A52. Some people make light of this. ~ GAS
A54. This is able to work its way in anywhere on account of its form. ~ WEDGE
A55. This is long in detail. ~ RAT
A57. These are quite gushing. ~ SPURTS
A60. This is Margaret before she grew up. ~ MEG
A61. What bride's toast usually does (sing.) ~ BURN
A63. This is what we all wish we had. ~ ASSETS
A65. This is what they did at the meeting. ~ MET
A67. Generally speaking this is what a Chinaman is. ~ ASIAN
A69. This is always running around a circus tent. ~ ROPE
A71. An encore for Hor. No. 45. ~ DO
A72. A member of a set in China. ~ TEAPOT
A74. You may get this in the next world. ~ REWARD
A76. The kind of old age that is welcome. ~ HALE
A77. You will have to do some brilliant head work when you get down to these (two words). ~ BRASSTACKS

D1. This is not a wide awake state. ~ COMA
D2. This is way back in history. (abbr.) ~ BC
D3. This is never high even in a bull market. ~ LOW
D4. A good come back. ~ ECHO
D5. These are what the Fords, Dodges and Chryslers are. ~ GOERS
D6. What a revolver can do. ~ ROTATE
D7. An article that is often used before afternoon or evening. ~ AN
D8. This represents a portion of land that Col. Lindbergh overlooked last year. (abbr.) ~ SA
D9. A good thing to do to dry articles. ~ SKIP
D10. These rings are heavenly. ~ HALOS
D11. Timely warnings. ~ ALARMS
D12. The boss spends a lot of time talking to this one. ~ DICTAPHONE
D15. Better conform to the letter of the law here. (abbr.) ~ LL
D17. This show is intense. ~ CIRCUS
D21. Parking space. ~ ROOM
D23. These are all dated up a year ahead. ~ CALENDARS
D24. Giving excuses for staying down at the office. ~ LYING
D25. Consult an electrical engineer (abbr.). ~ EE
D28. The kind of literature found in a waste basket. ~ TRASHY
